=== Introduction
--- Psalms 31–32 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 31–32
sub: Into thine hand I commit my spirit • Blessed is he whose transgression is forgiven
refs: Psalm 31:5; Psalm 31:15; Psalm 32:1-2
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 31
--- Psalm 31 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 31
sub: The LORD God of truth • My times are in thy hand
refs: Psalm 31:1

--- Father, into thy hands
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 31:5
big: Luke 23:46 | mark: Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit
p: David's Psalms of trust in the Lord God, to be delivered and never ashamed, redeemed in His righteousness, are models of prayer, and Jesus upon the cross quotes them (or rather, they quote Him). How much more should we?

--- My rock and my fortress
kicker: Psalm 31 · In thee, O Lord
scripture: Psalm 31:1-4 | mark: let me never be ashamed; For thou art my rock and my fortress
p: The cries to listen to our prayers and save us out of trouble, acknowledging that He is our rock and our fortress, our Savior, our ready help in time of tribulation, they echo through time and are as relevant to Christians today as they were to the King of Israel, and the congregation that sang them 3,000 years ago.

--- O Lord God of truth
kicker: Psalm 31 · Into thine hand
big: Psalm 31:5 | mark: Into thine hand I commit my spirit; O LORD God of truth
p: Trusting the God of truth to keep His word, having faith in His promises, is the foundation for our Christian life. Relying on His word to feed us with truth. Knowing the truth that sets us free.
p: And loving the truth puts us in a position where we hate those that lie about the Lord.

--- I hate every false way
kicker: Psalm 31 · Lying vanities
big: Psalm 31:6 | mark: I have hated them that regard lying vanities
big: Psalm 119:104 | mark: therefore I hate every false way
p: This love of the truth and understanding of His word is a gift of the Spirit of God.
big: 1 John 4:6 | mark: the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error

--- Doctrines of devils
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 31:6
p: All things are made known to us by the Spirit, through Christ who is the way, the truth, the light.
p: And so we should be vexed by false prophets who teach damnable heresy.
scripture: 1 Timothy 4:1-2 | mark: doctrines of devils
p: These false prophets are reprobates concerning the faith. Their lies make their followers twofold more the child of Hell than themselves. They are enemies of God, and we should hate them with a perfect hatred, while loving those misled by them enough to proclaim the truth.

--- A large room
kicker: Psalm 31 · Thy mercy
scripture: Psalm 31:7-8 | mark: I will be glad and rejoice in thy mercy
p: Continuing in **Psalm 31**, picking up in verse 9. The gladness and rejoicing in God's mercy and crying out to Him in the earlier stanzas, brings us the heart of the matter, the trouble in David's life, and reflected in our own struggles.

--- I am in trouble
kicker: Psalm 31 · Have mercy upon me
big: Psalm 31:9 | mark: for I am in trouble
p: He says I am in trouble, consumed with grief and physically and spiritually affected by the tribulation in his life. This is something everyone alive can relate to. Anyone alive long enough to be broken hearted. Betrayed. Lied about and persecuted.

--- My life is spent with grief
kicker: Psalm 31 · Grief
big: Psalm 31:10 | mark: my life is spent with grief
p: When we are going through it, it can feel as though all we ever have known is grief. We can feel all alone, when not only our enemies abandon us, but our neighbors and friends won't even look at us. So miserable and alone.

--- Forgotten as a dead man
kicker: Psalm 31 · A broken vessel
scripture: Psalm 31:11-13 | mark: I am forgotten as a dead man out of mind
p: We may feel forgotten as a dead man, out of sight out of mind. Truly miserable. Feeling as though the whole world has conspired against us. Having nowhere to turn or anyone to trust.

--- My times are in thy hand
kicker: Psalm 31 · Thou art my God
scripture: Psalm 31:14-16 | mark: But I trusted in thee, O LORD: I said, Thou art my God; My times are in thy hand
p: *But I trusted in thee, O LORD: I said, Thou art my God.* What a beautiful reminder that we have a friend in Jesus. We have a Lord who is trustworthy and Who cares. Whose face shines on us, and saves us. Not because of how good we are, but despite our sin. For His mercies' sake. Because He so loved us.

--- Let the wicked be ashamed
kicker: Psalm 31 · Let me not be ashamed
big: Psalm 31:17
p: We plead, *Let me not be ashamed, O LORD; for I have called upon thee*, and we know that God, Who cannot lie, has promised us deliverance. *Let the wicked be ashamed, and let them be silent in the grave.* We will sing His praise forever, while they are tormented by unquenchable fire, where the worm dieth not.

--- Where their worm dieth not
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 31:17
big: Isaiah 66:24 | mark: their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched
scripture: Mark 9:47-49 | mark: Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched

--- Lying lips
kicker: Psalm 31 · Put to silence
p: All those that have denied the truth of God shall be silenced. They shall be made to be ashamed. Let the wicked be ashamed, but we have trusted the Lord.
big: Psalm 31:18 | mark: Let the lying lips be put to silence

--- Oh how great is thy goodness
kicker: Psalm 31 · Thy goodness
big: Psalm 31:19 | mark: Oh how great is thy goodness
p: The culmination of the Psalm is a tribute to the richness of God's mercy and fidelity in times of trouble, and the sure destruction of those that hate Him.

--- The strife of tongues
kicker: Psalm 31 · Thy marvellous kindness
scripture: Psalm 31:20-22 | mark: from the strife of tongues; For I said in my haste, I am cut off from before thine eyes
p: God shall hide those that trust in Him from the wrath to come. Shield us from the wicked, guard us from the strife of their tongues, their slander and railing will come to nothing.
p: Bless the Lord for His marvelous kindness. For the protection He has granted those that trust in Him. Even when we feel alone and desperate. Even the great prophet, Elijah, fresh from a great work for God, and a spiritual highpoint, felt alone and in his haste thought he was cut off from before God's eyes.

--- I have reserved to myself
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 31:22
scripture: Romans 11:3-4 | mark: I have reserved to myself seven thousand men
p: We might have moments of doubt, when God's perfect timing isn't lining up with our own. When we think we cannot go on. That there are Jezebels and her men after us. That we are all alone. But God has reserved His own. We will be preserved. We can comfort ourselves and others with this truth.

--- O love the Lord, all ye his saints
kicker: Psalm 31 · Be of good courage
scripture: Psalm 31:23-24 | mark: O love the LORD, all ye his saints; Be of good courage
center: Amen.

=== Psalm 32
--- Psalm 32 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 32
sub: The blessedness of forgiveness
refs: Psalm 32:1

--- Whose transgression is forgiven
kicker: Psalm 32 · Blessed
scripture: Psalm 32:1-2 | mark: Blessed is he whose transgression is forgiven, whose sin is covered; unto whom the LORD imputeth not iniquity
p: **Psalm 32** opens with a very familiar and important doctrine, the understanding of God's forgiveness for sin in the saved believer's life. This foundational fact is repeated by Paul in the letter to the Romans;

--- Righteousness without works
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 32:1–2
scripture: Romans 4:6-8 | mark: unto whom God imputeth righteousness without works
p: The truth is that, in Christ, whether it was in David's day, or Paul's or our own, God forgives our past transgressions, covers our current sin, and will not hold our future sins and iniquity against us.

--- No guile
kicker: Psalm 32 · The new man
p: This positional standing of righteousness before God, despite our sins, through His imputed righteousness is made possible, through the Holy Spirit, by the renewal, the regeneration, the quickening of our spirit. The new man within us. Blessed is the man *in whose spirit there is no guile.*
p: We are given the Spirit of truth, that we may acknowledge our sin, knowing that we are forgiven of all sin, first, not by works but by faith.
p: The same good news today as in David's day. And this Psalm serves as an evangelical appeal.

--- When I kept silence
kicker: Psalm 32 · Thy hand was heavy
scripture: Psalm 32:3-4 | mark: When I kept silence, my bones waxed old
p: David writes of a man who kept his silence, who hadn't called upon the name of the Lord, who suffered in their sins, bones waxing old and dried up by their roaring in the daytime, the heaviness of their iniquity weighing on them at night.

--- I acknowledged my sin
kicker: Psalm 32 · Confession
big: Psalm 32:5 | mark: I acknowledged my sin unto thee; thou forgavest the iniquity of my sin
p: But the honesty and humility of acknowledging sin, not hiding anything from God (as if we could), confessing our transgressions unto the Lord, and being forgiven, is a blessing. Roaring in the daytime and being kept up at night by our conscience, being convicted in the Spirit, gives way to the peace that passes understanding, through the forgiveness that is given to us by God's mercy and grace.

--- While he may be found
kicker: Psalm 32 · A time when thou mayest be found
big: Psalm 32:6 | mark: in a time when thou mayest be found
p: David reminds us that there is a time where God may be sought, before it is too late.
big: Isaiah 55:6 | mark: Seek ye the LORD while he may be found
p: God's Spirit will not always strive with man. There comes a time when those that put off or reject the truth, not acknowledging that they are sinners, lying to God and themselves, are no longer able to come to Him. Where He no longer extends His hand, because they have hardened their hearts and will not seek Him, even as they face destruction.

--- To day if ye will hear his voice
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 32:6
big: Hebrews 4:7 | mark: To day if ye will hear his voice, harden not your hearts
p: We have the example of those in the days of Noah, who mocked him for his faith in preparing the ark, and whose disobedient spirits who Christ preached to in Hell, were destroyed by the flood, never coming nigh unto God. Just as those left to God's wrath to come will not repent, despite the horrors of His judgment.

--- Now is the day of salvation
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 32:6
p: There is a time, it is today. There is a day, and it is the present. If you haven't accepted the truth that we are sinners in need of salvation, and that it is offered as a free gift to all who believe, through the grace of Almighty God, now is the time.
big: 2 Corinthians 6:2 | mark: now is the day of salvation

--- Thou art my hiding place
kicker: Psalm 32 · Songs of deliverance
p: Those who have heard and responded to the truth, who in the appointed day prayed unto Him, can rest assured that we are saved.
big: Psalm 32:7 | mark: Thou art my hiding place
p: This is the hope we have. The same hope the congregations of the children of Israel sang of in the temple of Solomon, with the songs of his father echoing this blessed promise, centuries before the first coming of Christ Jesus.

--- Not as the horse, or as the mule
kicker: Psalm 32 · I will guide thee
scripture: Psalm 32:8-9 | mark: I will instruct thee and teach thee; Be ye not as the horse, or as the mule
p: And God responds to His children, in this inspired Psalm. Promising to teach and guide us in His ways. He asks we come willingly. That we have understanding to walk with Him in His ways, and not be like the horse or mule, needing a bit and bridle to pull us along. His yoke is light. The burden is easy.

--- Mercy shall compass him about
kicker: Psalm 32 · He that trusteth in the Lord
big: Psalm 32:10 | mark: he that trusteth in the LORD, mercy shall compass him about
p: The wicked may suffer their sorrows of their own making, but we who are forgiven by His mercy because we trust in the Lord, are preserved. What an amazing relief to know we are already forgiven. That we may come boldly before God to sing His praises, glorifying Him for His mercies' sake.

--- Shout for joy
kicker: Psalm 32 · Rejoice, ye righteous
big: Psalm 32:11
center: Amen!

--- Transgression
kicker: Psalm 32 · Transgression, sin and iniquity
p: While it isn't entirely wrong to take the words transgression, sin and iniquity, and use them interchangeably, they do carry subtle differences in their meaning.
p: Transgression is the wilful rebellion against the truth of God and His commands. The transgression of Adam, for example, having one law to obey, and choosing of their own volition (even as Eve was deceived, Adam most assuredly knew better), deciding to be disobedient.

--- Sin
kicker: Psalm 32 · Transgression, sin and iniquity
p: Sin, which the Bible says is the transgression of the Law, also carries with it the idea of missing the mark, falling short.
big: Romans 3:23
p: We aren't able, in the flesh, to hit the mark, the target being the perfect holiness of God. Only Jesus Christ did.
big: 2 Corinthians 5:21 | mark: who knew no sin

--- Iniquity
kicker: Psalm 32 · Transgression, sin and iniquity
p: Iniquity comes from the Hebrew word, *Avon*, meaning to twist, bend, or distort. The perverted and crooked nature of fallen man. The bent toward evil, the guilt that stays with us, and the perverted character that drives the outward actions. It is the *guile* or deceit mentioned in verse 2.
p: Our natural tendencies towards willful rebellion, to sin, and to lie to ourselves and others, including God, about the evil that we do. Excusing ourselves with lies.

--- Wounded for our transgressions
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 32:1–2
p: Taken together, the blessed assurance that our transgressions, sins and iniquities are eternally forgiven demonstrates the mercy and grace of our Lord towards His children, through the suffering of Jesus.
scripture: Isaiah 53:5-6 | mark: he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities

--- The ledger
kicker: Psalm 32 · Justified in Christ
p: He suffered these things for our iniquity and transgressions, that we may be purged. That His righteousness be imputed on our ledger, even as His blood blots away our sins.
p: Our transgressions are forgiven, our sin is covered, our iniquity will not be added to our account, because we are justified in Christ.
p: This accounting is a spiritual gift, applied to the ledger that in judgment, we are found not guilty by God.

--- Past, present and future
kicker: Psalm 32 · Justified in Christ
p: (Some call this Penal Substitutionary Atonement, the idea that Jesus paid the price of the debt we owe, so that we do not have to receive the wages of sin. Many reject this idea. Whatever you want to call it, it is undeniable that Christ suffered that those who trust on Him, won't)
p: On the cross, Jesus carried away our rebellion, covered our failures with His blood, and cleared the ledger of our crooked nature by imputing His own perfect righteousness to us. Past, present and future. That we may be made the righteousness of God.

--- The day of atonement
kicker: Cross references • Leviticus 16
scripture: Leviticus 16:21-22 | mark: confess over him all the iniquities of the children of Israel, and all their transgressions in all their sins
big: Leviticus 16:30 | mark: that ye may be clean from all your sins before the LORD
center: [[Leviticus 16:2-30|Read Leviticus 16:2–30]]

--- One sacrifice for all time
kicker: Cross references • Leviticus 16
p: This concept of atonement by the sacrifice, the scapegoat and the blood, is established in **Leviticus 16.**
p: Rather than the picture of the imperfect sacrifice, the blood of bulls and goats which could never take away our sin, we have the flawless Lamb, Who is also the ultimate High Priest, and one sacrifice for all time.
scripture: Hebrews 10:10-14 | mark: once for all; one sacrifice for sins for ever

--- The work is finished
kicker: Psalm 32 · Covered
p: He had no sin of His own to sacrifice for. He did not enter a man-made temple, but Heaven itself. He did not offer the blood of animals, but His own flawless blood.
p: Because of what Jesus did as both the High Priest and the Sacrifice, the work is finished. The ledger is cleared permanently. God no longer imputes our iniquity, our sins are eternally covered, and our transgressions have been carried as far as the east is from the west. And God will remember them no more.

--- Every priest standeth daily
kicker: Cross references • Hebrews 10
big: Hebrews 10:11 | mark: every priest standeth daily
p: In the physical Tabernacle and the Temple of Solomon, there were no chairs. A priest could never sit down because his job was never done. The blood of bulls and goats only provided a temporary cover for the *remembrance of sins made again every year*, meaning the priest had to stay on his feet, continuously working, because he could never truly take away sins.

--- He sat down
kicker: Cross references • Hebrews 10
p: But then we see Jesus, the author and most importantly, the finisher of our faith, sits down, on the right hand of the Father.
scripture: Hebrews 10:12-14 | mark: sat down on the right hand of God
p: The contrast of those who stood, continually ministering, versus Jesus, Who sat down forever, having perfected them that are sanctified, is a subtle but crucial detail. An interesting side note to the study, that we shouldn't overlook.

